        "---\n",
        "## **1. What Does It Mean to Understand Language?**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Neural networks can model many more kinds of data than images, and each new kind of data teaches us something about networks.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The chapter begins with two questions: what does it mean to understand language, and what kinds of predictions do people make about it? So far, neural networks have been applied to image data, but they can be used on a far wider variety of datasets. Exploring new datasets is instructive in itself, because different datasets tend to call for different styles of training, depending on the challenges hidden in the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "> A map of related fields: natural language processing (NLP) overlaps deep learning, machine learning, and artificial intelligence, alongside image recognition\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.1.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.1\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "The chapter starts with natural language processing, a much older field that overlaps with deep learning. NLP is devoted entirely to the automated understanding of human language, and it did not originally rely on deep learning. The sections that follow discuss the basics of how deep learning approaches this field."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-s2"
      },
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## **2. Natural Language Processing (NLP)**\n",
        "\n",
        "**NLP is divided into a collection of tasks or challenges.**\n",
        "\n",
        "A quick way to get to know NLP is to look at some of the problems its community tries to solve. Typical classification problems include:\n",
        "\n",
        "- using the **characters** of a document to predict where words start and end;\n",
        "- using the **words** of a document to predict where sentences start and end;\n",
        "- using the **words in a sentence** to predict the part of speech of each word;\n",
        "- using the **words in a sentence** to predict where phrases start and end;\n",
        "- using the **words in a sentence** to predict where references to named entities (a person, place, or thing) start and end;\n",
        "- using the **sentences in a document** to predict which pronouns refer to the same person, place, or thing;\n",
        "- using the **words in a sentence** to predict the **sentiment** of the sentence.\n",
        "\n",
        "In general, an NLP task aims to do one of three things:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Kind of task | Examples |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| **Label** a region of text | part-of-speech tagging, sentiment classification, named-entity recognition |\n",
        "| **Link** two or more regions of text | coreference, which asks whether two mentions of a real-world thing (usually a person, place, or other named entity) refer to the same thing |\n",
        "| **Fill in** missing information (missing words) based on context | predicting an omitted word |\n",
        "\n",
        "Machine learning and NLP are closely intertwined. Until recently, most state-of-the-art NLP algorithms were advanced probabilistic, non-parametric models rather than deep learning. Two neural techniques have since swept through the field: **neural word embeddings** and **recurrent neural networks (RNNs)**. This chapter builds a word-embedding algorithm and shows why it improves the accuracy of NLP methods; the next chapter builds a recurrent neural network and shows why it predicts so well across sequences.\n",
        "\n",
        "NLP also plays a special part in the advancement of artificial intelligence. AI aims at machines that can think and engage with the world as humans do (and beyond), and language is the foundation of conscious logic and communication in people. Methods that let machines use and understand language therefore form the basis of human-like logic in machines, which the book calls \"the foundation of thought\"."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-s3"
      },
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## **3. Supervised NLP**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Words go in, and predictions come out.**\n",
        "\n",
        "Chapter 2 presented supervised learning as the transformation of \"what you know\" into \"what you want to know\".\n",
        "\n",
        "> Supervised learning: from what you know to what you want to know\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.2.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.2\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "In all earlier examples, \"what you know\" consisted of numbers in one form or another. NLP, however, starts from **text**. Since neural networks only map input numbers to output numbers, the first step is to convert the text into a numerical form, in much the same way that the streetlight data was turned into a matrix. How this conversion is done turns out to be very important.\n",
        "\n",
        "> For text, a conversion step is needed: raw text becomes a matrix of numbers before supervised learning\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.3.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.3\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "The guiding principle is that a neural network looks for **correlation** between its input and output layers. The text should therefore be converted into numbers so that this correlation is as **obvious** as possible to the network, which results in faster training and better generalization. To decide which input format achieves this, one has to know what the input and output data look like. The book explores the question through a classification challenge, which it introduces as topic classification; the concrete task that follows is predicting the sentiment of movie reviews."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s3"
    },
