        "---\n",
        "## **4. The IMDB Movie Reviews Dataset**\n",
        "\n",
        "**You can predict whether people post positive or negative reviews.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The IMDB movie reviews dataset consists of review-to-rating pairs. The book gives an imitation (not taken from IMDB) to show the form: the review \"This movie was terrible! The plot was dry, the acting unconvincing, and I spilled popcorn on my shirt.\" carries the rating 1 star.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataset is described as containing around 50,000 such pairs. The reviews are usually a few sentences long, and the ratings range from 1 to 5 stars. It is regarded as a **sentiment dataset**, because the stars indicate the overall sentiment of a review. The book cautions that such a dataset may differ greatly from other sentiment datasets, such as product reviews or hospital patient reviews.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal is a network that uses the review text to predict the score. This requires turning both the inputs and the outputs into matrices.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Output.** The rating is already a number, so it is the easier part. The range of 1 to 5 stars is rescaled to the range 0 to 1, so that the book's \"binary softmax\" output can be used. Nothing else is needed.\n",
        "- **Input.** The raw input is a list of characters, which creates two problems: it is text rather than numbers, and it has **variable length**, whereas the networks so far always took inputs of a fixed size.\n",
        "\n",
        "Since the raw input cannot be used directly, the next question is which property of the data correlates with the output. Individual characters should hardly correlate with sentiment. **Words** are different: the book expects words such as *terrible* and *unconvincing* to correlate significantly and negatively with the rating, which means that the more often they occur in a review, the lower the rating tends to be. Perhaps this is a general property, and words taken on their own, even out of context, correlate with sentiment. The next section explores this idea."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-s5"
      },
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## **5. Capturing Word Correlation in Input Data**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Bag of words: given a review's vocabulary, predict the sentiment.**\n",
        "\n",
        "If the vocabulary of a review does correlate with its rating, the next step is an input matrix that represents the vocabulary of each review. The usual construction has one **row (vector) per review** and one **column per word** of the vocabulary. A column holds 1 when the review contains the corresponding word and 0 otherwise. If there are 2,000 words, each vector has 2,000 dimensions.\n",
        "\n",
        "This storage format is called **one-hot encoding**: it is the most common way to encode the binary presence or absence of an item from a vocabulary of possible items. With a vocabulary of only four words, it looks as follows. Because every word has its own vector, simple vector addition produces a vector for any subset of the vocabulary, such as the words of a sentence.\n",
        "\n",
        "> One-hot vectors for a four-word vocabulary (cat, the, dog, sat) and the sum of the vectors for the sentence \"the cat sat\", which is [1, 1, 0, 1]\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.4.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.4\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "When several words are combined, repeated words can be handled in two ways. For the phrase \"cat cat cat\", one may add the vector for \"cat\" three times (giving `[3,0,0,0]`) or count the word once (giving `[1,0,0,0]`). The second option usually works better for language, and it is the one used in the code of the following sections.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Note: the book's listing builds the dictionary `onehots` but then reads from an undefined name, `word2hot`. The cell below uses `onehots` in those three places so that the code runs. This is the only change made to the book's code in this notebook.*"
      ],
      "id": "c11-s5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-code-onehot",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "b604c234-c84a-4a05-e0dc-a7a1a18141e5"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Sent Encoding:[1 1 0 1]\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "onehots = {}\n",
        "onehots['cat'] = np.array([1,0,0,0])\n",
        "onehots['the'] = np.array([0,1,0,0])\n",
        "onehots['dog'] = np.array([0,0,1,0])\n",
        "onehots['sat'] = np.array([0,0,0,1])\n",
        "\n",
        "sentence = ['the','cat','sat']\n",
        "x = onehots[sentence[0]] + \\\n",
        "    onehots[sentence[1]] + \\\n",
        "    onehots[sentence[2]]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Sent Encoding:\" + str(x))"
      ],
      "id": "c11-code-onehot"
