        "---\n",
        "## **6. Predicting Movie Reviews**\n",
        "\n",
        "**With the encoding strategy and the previous network, you can predict sentiment.**\n",
        "\n",
        "With this encoding, a vector can be built for every word in the sentiment dataset, and the previous two-layer network can be used to predict sentiment. The book strongly recommends trying this from memory first: open a new Jupyter notebook, load the dataset, build the one-hot vectors, and then build a network that predicts whether each review is positive or negative. It then shows its own preprocessing step.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **6.1 Data Required by the Code**\n",
        "\n",
        "The code below (and the fill-in-the-blank code later in the chapter) reads data from two text files that are opened by their plain names, so they must be present in the notebook's working directory:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `reviews.txt`: the movie reviews, one review per line (the code reads it with `readlines()` and splits each line at spaces);\n",
        "- `labels.txt`: one label per line, aligned with `reviews.txt`; a line equal to `positive` (followed by a line break) becomes the target 1, and every other line becomes the target 0.\n",
        "\n",
        "The book identifies these files as the IMDB movie-review data described in Section 4; it does not give a download location in this chapter. Both files are available in the book's public repository (`iamtrask/Grokking-Deep-Learning`), so the setup cell below, which is **not part of the book's code**, downloads them into the working directory. Run it once before the preprocessing cell (`reviews.txt` is large, roughly 34 MB). The fill-in-the-blank section uses only `reviews.txt`.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **6.2 What the Preprocessing Does**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. `raw_reviews` and `raw_labels` hold the lines of the two files.\n",
        "2. `tokens` contains, for each review, the **set** of its words, so repeated words count once.\n",
        "3. `vocab` is the list of all distinct non-empty words, and `word2index` maps each word to its position in that list.\n",
        "4. `input_dataset` holds, for each review, the list of the **indices** of its distinct words. This is a compact way of storing the 1s of the one-hot vector.\n",
        "5. `target_dataset` contains 1 for positive reviews and 0 for the others."
