      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## **7. Intro to an Embedding Layer**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Here's one more trick to make the network faster.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The diagram below shows the network from before, which will now predict sentiment. Its layers are named as follows: the first layer is the dataset (`layer_0`), followed by a **linear layer** (`weights_0_1`), then a layer of nonlinear units (`layer_1`, which the book calls a `relu` layer in this passage), another linear layer (`weights_1_2`), and finally the output, which is the prediction layer (`layer_2`).\n",
        "\n",
        "> The layers of the network: layer_0, weights_0_1, layer_1, weights_1_2, and the prediction layer_2\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.5.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.5\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "A shortcut is possible for `layer_1`: the first linear layer (`weights_0_1`) can be replaced by an **embedding layer**. Multiplying a vector of 1s and 0s by a matrix is mathematically equivalent to **summing several rows** of that matrix. The sentiment vocabulary has on the order of 70,000 words, so in a full vector-matrix multiplication most of the work consists of multiplying zeros by rows of the matrix before adding them. It is much more efficient to select the rows that belong to the words present and to add them (or average them).\n",
        "\n",
        "> The one-hot vector-matrix multiplication for \"the cat sat\": the input [1, 0, 1, 0] multiplies the whole weights_0_1 matrix to give layer_1\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.6.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.6\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "> The same layer_1 obtained by summing only the rows of weights_0_1 that belong to the words \"the\", \"sat\", and \"cat\"\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.7.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.7\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "Treating the first linear layer as a lookup of rows followed by a sum (or average) is what is meant by an embedding layer. Structurally the two methods are **identical**: `layer_1` has exactly the same values in forward propagation. The only difference is that summing a small number of rows is much faster.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **7.1 Training the Sentiment Network**\n",
        "\n",
        "The next listing is to be run after the preprocessing code (it needs `vocab`, `input_dataset`, `target_dataset`, and the `sys` module imported there). Its main elements, as annotated in the book, are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- training on the first 24,000 reviews (`len(input_dataset)-1000`), with the last 1,000 kept as a test set;\n",
        "- the embedding step with a sigmoid, `layer_1 = sigmoid(np.sum(weights_0_1[x],axis=0))`;\n",
        "- the linear step with the output nonlinearity, `layer_2 = sigmoid(np.dot(layer_1,weights_1_2))`, which the book's annotation labels \"linear + softmax\";\n",
        "- the comparison of the prediction with the truth (`layer_2_delta`) and backpropagation (`layer_1_delta`);\n",
        "- weight updates in which only the rows of `weights_0_1` belonging to the words of the current review are changed.\n",
        "\n",
        "A prediction counts as correct when it is within 0.5 of the target. The book reports the following output for the full dataset:\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "Iter:0 Progress:95.99% Training Accuracy:0.832%\n",
        "Iter:1 Progress:95.99% Training Accuracy:0.8663333333333333%\n",
        "Test Accuracy:0.849\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "(The accuracies are fractions, even though the program appends a percent sign to them.) That is, the network reaches about 83% training accuracy in the first pass, about 87% in the second, and a test accuracy of 0.849."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 4,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-code-train-sentiment",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "12dbff08-70f7-4a84-8753-eaf9bbd737e8"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Iter:0 Progress:95.99% Training Accuracy:0.828875%\n",
            "Iter:1 Progress:95.99% Training Accuracy:0.8642916666666667%\n",
            "Test Accuracy:0.849\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "np.random.seed(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "def sigmoid(x):\n",
        "    return 1/(1 + np.exp(-x))\n",
        "\n",
        "alpha, iterations = (0.01, 2)\n",
        "hidden_size = 100\n",
        "\n",
        "weights_0_1 = 0.2*np.random.random((len(vocab),hidden_size)) - 0.1\n",
        "weights_1_2 = 0.2*np.random.random((hidden_size,1)) - 0.1\n",
        "\n",
        "correct,total = (0,0)\n",
        "for iter in range(iterations):\n",
        "\n",
        "    for i in range(len(input_dataset)-1000):\n",
        "\n",
        "        x,y = (input_dataset[i],target_dataset[i])\n",
        "        layer_1 = sigmoid(np.sum(weights_0_1[x],axis=0))\n",
        "        layer_2 = sigmoid(np.dot(layer_1,weights_1_2))\n",
        "\n",
        "        layer_2_delta = layer_2 - y\n",
        "        layer_1_delta = layer_2_delta.dot(weights_1_2.T)\n",
        "\n",
        "        weights_0_1[x] -= layer_1_delta * alpha\n",
        "        weights_1_2 -= np.outer(layer_1,layer_2_delta) * alpha\n",
        "\n",
        "        if(np.abs(layer_2_delta) < 0.5):\n",
        "            correct += 1\n",
        "        total += 1\n",
        "        if(i % 10 == 9):\n",
        "            progress = str(i/float(len(input_dataset)))\n",
        "            sys.stdout.write('\\rIter:'+str(iter)\\\n",
        "                             +' Progress:'+progress[2:4]\\\n",
        "                             +'.'+progress[4:6]\\\n",
        "                             +'% Training Accuracy:'\\\n",
        "                             + str(correct/float(total)) + '%')\n",
        "    print()\n",
        "correct,total = (0,0)\n",
        "for i in range(len(input_dataset)-1000,len(input_dataset)):\n",
        "\n",
        "    x = input_dataset[i]\n",
        "    y = target_dataset[i]\n",
        "\n",
        "    layer_1 = sigmoid(np.sum(weights_0_1[x],axis=0))\n",
        "    layer_2 = sigmoid(np.dot(layer_1,weights_1_2))\n",
        "\n",
        "    if(np.abs(layer_2 - y) < 0.5):\n",
        "        correct += 1\n",
        "    total += 1\n",
        "print(\"Test Accuracy:\" + str(correct / float(total)))"
      ],
      "id": "c11-code-train-sentiment"
    },
