      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## **8. Interpreting the Output**\n",
        "\n",
        "**What did the neural network learn along the way?**\n",
        "\n",
        "From one point of view, the result is the same **correlation summarization** already discussed: the network searched for correlation between the input datapoints and the output datapoints. These datapoints, however, are language, so it is very useful to ask which patterns of language the correlation summarization would detect and, more importantly, which it would not. Finding correlation between input and output does not mean that the network understands every useful pattern of language. Comparing what the network in its present configuration *can* learn with what it would *need* to know to understand language is exactly the line of thinking pursued by researchers at the frontier.\n",
        "\n",
        "> The task as presented to the network: the review vocabulary goes in, and a positive/negative label comes out\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.8.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.8\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "> The sentiment network: an input layer, weights_0_1, a hidden layer, weights_1_2, and one output\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.9.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.9\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "What did the network learn about language? It was given the vocabulary of each review as input and asked to predict one of two labels, positive or negative. Since the correlation summarization looks for correlation between input and output, at the very least one expects the network to find words that are positively or negatively correlated with the label by themselves. The input records the presence or absence of each word, so the network will find a direct correlation between that presence or absence and each of the two labels. But this is not the whole story."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
