        "id": "c11-s9"
      },
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## **9. Neural Architecture**\n",
        "\n",
        "**How did the choice of architecture affect what the network learned?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Direct correlation between input and target data is the first and most trivial thing the network learned. It is largely the clean slate of neural intelligence: if a network cannot even discover direct correlation, something is probably broken. More sophisticated architectures exist because more complex patterns than direct correlation need to be found, and this network is no exception.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **9.1 What Does the Hidden Layer Do?**\n",
        "\n",
        "The minimal architecture for finding direct correlation is a **two-layer network**, with a single weight matrix from the input layer to the output layer. The network used here has a hidden layer, so what is it for?\n",
        "\n",
        "Fundamentally, a hidden layer **groups** the datapoints of the previous layer into *n* groups, where *n* is the number of hidden neurons. Each hidden neuron receives a datapoint and answers the question \"Is this datapoint in my group?\" During learning, the layer searches for useful groupings of its input. A grouping is useful if it satisfies two conditions:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **It helps to predict an output label.** If a grouping is useless for the prediction, the correlation summarization will never lead the network to find it. The book regards this as a hugely valuable realization: much of neural network research consists of finding training data (or another manufactured signal that the network is made to predict artificially) so that the network finds groupings useful for a task such as predicting movie review stars.\n",
        "2. **It reflects a real phenomenon in the data that we care about.** Bad groupings simply memorize the data, whereas good groupings capture phenomena that are useful linguistically.\n",
        "\n",
        "An example is **negation**. Distinguishing \"terrible\" from \"not terrible\" would be a powerful grouping when predicting whether a review is positive. One would like a neuron that turns *off* when it sees \"awful\" and *on* when it sees \"not awful\". However, the input is only the vocabulary of the review, so \"it was great, not terrible\" produces exactly the same `layer_1` value as \"it was terrible, not great\". Consequently, the network is very unlikely to create a hidden neuron that understands negation.\n",
        "\n",
        "This suggests a practical test. To judge whether an architecture is likely to find a language pattern through the correlation summarization, try to construct two examples with an **identical hidden layer**, one containing the pattern and one without it. If that is possible, the network is unlikely to find the pattern.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **9.2 Similarity of Datapoints and of Words**\n",
        "\n",
        "A hidden layer groups the data of the previous layer. At the finest level, each neuron decides whether a datapoint belongs to its group. At a higher level, two datapoints (movie reviews) are similar if they belong to many of the same groups. Finally, two inputs (words) are similar if the weights connecting them to the hidden neurons, which measure the word's affinity for each group, are similar. What should one therefore observe in the weights from the words to the hidden neurons?\n",
        "\n",
        "### **9.3 What Should You See in the Weights Connecting Words and Hidden Neurons?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Words with similar predictive power should belong to similar groups, that is, to similar hidden-neuron configurations. Hence **words that correlate with the same label (positive or negative) will have similar weights to the hidden neurons**: the network learns to place them in similar hidden neurons so that the last layer (`weights_1_2`) can predict the correct label. The effect can be seen by taking a strongly positive or negative word and searching for the words whose weights to every hidden neuron are most similar.\n",
        "\n",
        "> The weights leaving the word \"good\" (the bold arrows, annotated with values such as .15, .23, and -.30) form its embedding: they show how strongly \"good\" belongs to each group (hidden neuron), and words with similar predictive power have similar embeddings\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.10.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.10\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "Words that belong to similar groups have similar predictive power for the positive and negative labels and, because their weight values are alike, they also have similar *meaning*. Abstractly, **a neuron has a meaning similar to that of another neuron in the same layer if and only if it has similar weights to the next and/or previous layers**."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s9"
    },
