        "---\n",
        "## **11. What Is the Meaning of a Neuron?**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Meaning is entirely based on the target labels being predicted.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The groupings found by the network do not entirely match how a person would group the words. The word most similar to \"beautiful\" is \"atmosphere\". For deciding whether a review is positive or negative, the two words have almost identical meaning, whereas in the real world their meanings differ (one is an adjective and the other a noun, for example).\n",
        "\n",
        "This realization is very important: the meaning of a neuron in the network is defined by the **target labels**. Everything inside the network is interpreted in the light of the correlation summarization trying to make correct predictions. So, although we know a great deal about these words, the network knows nothing beyond the task at hand.\n",
        "\n",
        "How can the network be persuaded to learn more nuanced information about word neurons? By supplying input and target data that demand a more nuanced understanding of language, since then the network has a reason to learn finer interpretations of the words.\n",
        "\n",
        "What should the network predict so that the word neurons receive more interesting weights? The book proposes a \"glorified\" **fill-in-the-blank task**, for two reasons. First, there is nearly unlimited training data (the internet), and therefore nearly unlimited signal for learning nuanced information about words. Second, filling in a blank correctly requires at least some notion of real-world context. For example, in the sentence \"Mary had a little lamb whose ____ was white as snow\", is the missing word more likely to be \"anvil\" or \"wool\"? The next section lets the network try to find out."
      ],
