        "---\n",
        "## **12. Filling in the Blank**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Learn richer meanings for words by having a richer signal to learn.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The new example uses almost the same network as before, with a few modifications:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Instead of predicting one label for a whole movie review, each phrase has **one word removed** (the focus term), and the network is trained to identify the removed word from the rest of the phrase. The book describes these phrases as five words long.\n",
        "2. A trick called **negative sampling** is used to make training somewhat faster. Predicting the missing word requires one label for every possible word, that is, several thousand labels, which would make training slow. To avoid this, **most of the labels are randomly ignored in each forward-propagation step** (they are treated as if they did not exist). It may look like a crude approximation, but it works well in practice.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **12.1 Preprocessing**\n",
        "\n",
        "The preprocessing code differs from the previous one in the following ways. It needs only `reviews.txt` (see the data note in Section 6.1). The reviews are split into lists of words (not sets), a counter `wordcnt` holds a count for every word (decreased by one at each occurrence), and `vocab` and `word2index` are created from its words. `input_dataset` now keeps the word indices of each review in their original order, including repetitions, and `concatenated` is one long array of all word indices of all reviews (it is used below to draw random words for negative sampling). Finally, the order of the reviews is shuffled.\n",
        "\n",
        "*The cell reuses the names `vocab`, `word2index`, and `input_dataset` from the sentiment example and replaces them. The cells from here on therefore belong to the fill-in-the-blank network.*"
      ],
      "id": "c11-s12"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 7,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-code-fill-prep"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import sys,random,math\n",
        "from collections import Counter\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "np.random.seed(1)\n",
        "random.seed(1)\n",
        "f = open('reviews.txt')\n",
        "raw_reviews = f.readlines()\n",
        "f.close()\n",
        "\n",
        "tokens = list(map(lambda x:(x.split(\" \")),raw_reviews))\n",
        "wordcnt = Counter()\n",
        "for sent in tokens:\n",
        "    for word in sent:\n",
        "        wordcnt[word] -= 1\n",
        "vocab = list(set(map(lambda x:x[0],wordcnt.most_common())))\n",
        "\n",
        "word2index = {}\n",
        "for i,word in enumerate(vocab):\n",
        "    word2index[word]=i\n",
        "\n",
        "concatenated = list()\n",
        "input_dataset = list()\n",
        "for sent in tokens:\n",
        "    sent_indices = list()\n",
        "    for word in sent:\n",
        "        try:\n",
        "            sent_indices.append(word2index[word])\n",
        "            concatenated.append(word2index[word])\n",
        "        except:\n",
        "            \"\"\n",
        "    input_dataset.append(sent_indices)\n",
        "concatenated = np.array(concatenated)\n",
        "\n",
        "random.shuffle(input_dataset)"
      ],
      "id": "c11-code-fill-prep"
