        "### **13.1 Intelligence Targeting**\n",
        "\n",
        "The key point is that the network was trained on the same dataset with a very similar architecture (three layers, cross entropy, sigmoid nonlinearity), and yet what it stores in its weights can be influenced by **changing what the network is asked to predict**. It examines the same statistical information, but by choosing the input and target values one can steer what it learns. The book calls this process **intelligence targeting**.\n",
        "\n",
        "Choosing the inputs and targets is not the only way to do this. One can also change how the network measures error, the size and kind of its layers, and the regularization applied. In deep learning research, all of these techniques belong to the construction of a **loss function**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **13.2 Neural Networks Do Not Really Learn Data; They Minimize the Loss Function**\n",
        "\n",
        "This section re-explains learning from a different angle: choosing the error so that the network learns the patterns we are interested in. Chapter 4 taught two lessons, repeated in the book's box:\n",
        "\n",
        "> A recap of two lessons from Chapter 4: the golden method for learning (adjust each weight in the correct direction and by the correct amount so that the error reaches 0) and the secret (for any input and goal_pred there is an exact relationship between error and weight), with the formula error = ((0.5 * weight) - 0.8) ** 2\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.11.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.11\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "The formula `error = ((0.5 * weight) - 0.8) ** 2` comes from the one-weight network, where the error was obtained by forward propagating (`0.5 * weight`) and comparing the result with the target (0.8). The book encourages us **not** to see this as two separate steps (forward propagation and then error evaluation), but to treat the whole formula, forward propagation included, as the evaluation of an error value. This view reveals the true reason for the different word clusterings: although the two networks and their datasets were similar, the error function was fundamentally different.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **13.3 The Choice of Loss Function Determines the Network's Knowledge**\n",
        "\n",
        "An error function is more formally called a **loss function** or **objective function**; the three terms are interchangeable. Regarding learning as the minimization of a loss function, which includes forward propagation, gives a much broader view of how networks learn. Two networks may have identical initial weights and be trained on identical data, and yet learn very different patterns because they use different loss functions. For the two movie-review networks, the loss functions differed because the target values differed (positive or negative versus fill in the blank).\n",
        "\n",
        "Architectures, layer types, regularization techniques, datasets, and nonlinearities are therefore not so different from one another: they are all ways of constructing a loss function. If a network does not learn properly, the remedy can come from any of these categories. For instance, if a network overfits, the loss function can be altered by choosing simpler nonlinearities, smaller layers, shallower architectures, larger datasets, or stronger regularization, all of which have a fundamentally similar effect on the loss function and on the network's behavior. They interact, and over time one learns how changing one affects the others. For now, the main lesson is that **learning means constructing a loss function and then minimizing it**.\n",
        "\n",
        "Whenever you want a network to learn a pattern, everything needed is contained in the loss function. With a single weight the loss function was simple, as the formula above shows. When many complex layers are chained, the loss function becomes more complicated, which is acceptable. If something goes wrong, the solution lies in the loss function, which covers both the forward prediction and the raw error evaluation (such as mean squared error or cross entropy)."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s13b"
    },
