        "---\n",
        "## **14. King – Man + Woman ~= Queen**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Word analogies are an interesting consequence of the network built before.**\n",
        "\n",
        "Before the chapter closes, the book discusses what is still one of the most famous properties of neural word embeddings (word vectors such as the ones just created). Filling in the blank produces embeddings with an interesting property, known as **word analogies**: the vectors of different words can be combined with basic algebra. If the previous network is trained on a large enough corpus, one can take the vector of `king`, subtract the vector of `man`, add the vector of `woman`, and look for the vector most similar to the result (other than those in the query). The most similar vector is often the word \"queen\". The book adds that similar phenomena occur in the fill-in-the-blank network trained on the movie reviews.\n",
        "\n",
        "The function `analogy` implements this. It takes lists of `positive` and `negative` words. It computes the squared length of each word vector (`norms`), scales the vectors by it (`normed_weights`), adds the vectors of the positive words, subtracts those of the negative words (`query_vect`), and then ranks all words by their negative Euclidean distance to the query. The best-ranked entry is dropped (`[1:]`), which leaves nine results. The cell must be run after the fill-in-the-blank training, because it uses the trained `weights_0_1`."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s14"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 10,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c11-code-analogy-def"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def analogy(positive=['terrible','good'],negative=['bad']):\n",
        "\n",
        "    norms = np.sum(weights_0_1 * weights_0_1,axis=1)\n",
        "    norms.resize(norms.shape[0],1)\n",
        "\n",
        "    normed_weights = weights_0_1 * norms\n",
        "\n",
        "    query_vect = np.zeros(len(weights_0_1[0]))\n",
        "    for word in positive:\n",
        "        query_vect += normed_weights[word2index[word]]\n",
        "    for word in negative:\n",
        "        query_vect -= normed_weights[word2index[word]]\n",
        "\n",
        "    scores = Counter()\n",
        "    for word,index in word2index.items():\n",
        "        raw_difference = weights_0_1[index] - query_vect\n",
        "        squared_difference = raw_difference * raw_difference\n",
        "        scores[word] = -math.sqrt(sum(squared_difference))\n",
        "\n",
        "    return scores.most_common(10)[1:]"
      ],
