        "---\n",
        "## **15. Word Analogies**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Linear compression of an existing property in the data.**\n",
        "\n",
        "When the word-analogy property was first discovered, it caused a wave of excitement, and people imagined many applications. It is an amazing property in its own right and it spawned a veritable cottage industry around producing word embeddings of one kind or another. Since then, however, the analogy property itself has not developed much, and most current language work concentrates instead on **recurrent architectures**, which the next chapter introduces.\n",
        "\n",
        "Even so, a good intuition for what happens in word embeddings as a result of a chosen loss function is very valuable. The choice of loss function affects how words are grouped, as seen above, but the analogy phenomenon is something different. What about the new loss function causes it?\n",
        "\n",
        "### **15.1 A Two-Dimensional Example**\n",
        "\n",
        "The effect is easiest to imagine with a word embedding of only two dimensions. The book uses these vectors:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Word | Vector |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| king | [0.6, 0.1] |\n",
        "| man | [0.5, 0.0] |\n",
        "| woman | [0.0, 0.8] |\n",
        "| queen | [0.1, 1.0] |\n",
        "\n",
        "The differences are `king - man = [0.1, 0.1]` and `queen - woman = [0.1, 0.2]`. They are similar, and a simple addition gives `king - man + woman = [0.1, 0.9]`, which lies close to `queen`.\n",
        "\n",
        "> Moving from king to man, then from man to woman, lands near queen: king – man + woman ==\n",
        ">\n",
        "> <img src=\"pictures/chapter 11/picture 11.12.png\" alt=\"Figure 11.12\" width=\"600\"/>\n",
        "\n",
        "### **15.2 Why It Works**\n",
        "\n",
        "The relative usefulness of \"king\" and \"man\" for the final prediction is similar to that of \"queen\" and \"woman\". The difference between \"king\" and \"man\" leaves a vector of **royalty**. There is a group of male- and female-related words in one grouping, and another grouping lies along the royal direction.\n",
        "\n",
        "This can be traced back to the chosen loss. When \"king\" appears in a phrase, it changes the probability of other words appearing in a particular way: it raises the probability of words related to \"man\" and of words related to royalty. When \"queen\" appears, it raises the probability of words related to \"woman\" and, as a group, of words related to royalty. Because the words have this kind of Venn-diagram effect on the output probabilities, they end up belonging to similar combinations of groupings.\n",
        "\n",
        "Put in an oversimplified way, \"king\" belongs to the male and royal dimensions of the hidden layer, while \"queen\" belongs to the female and royal dimensions. If we take the vector of \"king\", remove an approximation of the male dimensions, and add the female ones, we obtain something close to \"queen\". The most important takeaway is that this is **more a property of language than of deep learning**: any linear compression of these co-occurrence statistics will behave in a similar way."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s15"
    },
