        "---\n",
        "## **16. Summary**\n",
        "\n",
        "**This chapter covered neural word embeddings and the impact of the loss on learning.**\n",
        "\n",
        "- **NLP** is organized around tasks that label regions of text, link regions of text, or fill in missing information; two neural techniques, **word embeddings** and **recurrent neural networks**, have transformed it.\n",
        "- Neural networks only map numbers to numbers, so text has to be converted into a matrix that makes the **correlation** between input and output as obvious as possible. For sentiment prediction on movie reviews, the **vocabulary** of a review is encoded as a **one-hot** (bag-of-words) vector, and repeated words are counted once.\n",
        "- Multiplying a one-hot vector by a weight matrix equals **summing the rows** of the words present, so the first layer can be implemented as an **embedding layer**, which is much faster for a vocabulary of tens of thousands of words.\n",
        "- The sentiment network learns the **direct correlation** between words and labels. Hidden neurons act as **groups**, and a grouping is useful only if it helps to predict the label and reflects a real phenomenon in the data. A network whose input is only the review vocabulary cannot easily learn patterns such as negation, because different word orders produce an identical hidden layer.\n",
        "- The weights leaving a word form its **embedding**. Words are compared through the **Euclidean distance** between their rows of `weights_0_1`. In the sentiment network, words cluster by the label they tend to predict, so a word may be \"close\" to another one that is very different in the real world (for example \"beautiful\" and \"atmosphere\").\n",
        "- **The meaning of a neuron is defined by the target labels.** To learn more nuanced word meanings, the network needs a task that demands them, such as **filling in a blank**, which has nearly unlimited training data. **Negative sampling** speeds up this task by predicting only the true word and a few random words.\n",
        "- The fill-in-the-blank network clusters words by their tendency to **occur in the same phrases**, regardless of sentiment. Changing the targets changes what is learned from the same data: this is **intelligence targeting**.\n",
        "- Networks do not simply learn data; they **minimize a loss function** that includes forward propagation and the error measure. Targets, architecture, layer types, regularization, datasets, and nonlinearities are all ways of constructing the loss function, and the solution to a learning problem is to be found there.\n",
        "- The fill-in-the-blank embeddings support **word analogies** such as *king – man + woman ~= queen*. These arise because related words belong to combinations of groupings (for example male + royal versus female + royal). This is a property of language, as any linear compression of co-occurrence statistics should behave similarly, rather than of deep learning alone.\n",
        "- The author encourages the reader to build the chapter's examples from scratch, because the lessons about creating and tuning loss functions will be essential for the more complicated strategies of the following chapters."
      ],
      "id": "c11-s16"
